<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · period</p><h1 class="enc-title">Prehistory</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as pre-literary history</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Prehistory is the period of human history between the first known use of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stone-tools">stone tools</a> by hominins c. 3.3 million years ago and the beginning of recorded history with the invention of writing. The earliest known writing systems appeared c. 5,200 years ago, but writing spread gradually, so prehistory ended at different times in different places; the time when a culture is written about by others but has no writing of its own is often called its protohistory. Known only from archaeological and anthropological evidence, prehistory deals with archaeological cultures rather than named nations or individuals. The word first appeared in English in 1836.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The notion of prehistory emerged during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a> in the work of antiquarians, who used the word &quot;primitive&quot; for societies that existed before written records. During the nineteenth century British, French, German and Scandinavian anthropologists, archaeologists and antiquarians systematised the geologic time scale for pre-human periods and the three-age system for human prehistory. The most common view today is that history rests on a wide range of evidence; some historians remain uncomfortable with the term, which has been questioned but not discarded, and archaeologists regard it as a necessary distinction of the work they do.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Prehistory deals with archaeological cultures rather than named nations or individuals, and it is restricted to material processes, remains and artefacts. Its beginning and end vary by region. African prehistory begins c. 2.5 million years ago, Asian prehistory 1.8 million years ago and European prehistory c. 1.3 million years ago, with the first arrival of humans. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> it is generally taken to end around 3100 BCE, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-guinea">New Guinea</a> in the 1870s, when the Russian anthropologist Nicholai Miklukho-Maklai described the way of life of the native peoples, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a> in 1788.</p><p>In much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eurasia">Eurasia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> the period is divided by the three-age system into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stone-age">Stone Age</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a>, with a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalcolithic">Chalcolithic</a> or Copper Age as a transition in some areas. The system is not generally used in Oceania, Australasia, much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sub-saharan-africa">Sub-Saharan Africa</a> and parts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>, where hard metals arrived abruptly through contact with Eurasian cultures. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumer">Sumer</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus-valley-civilisation">Indus Valley Civilisation</a> and ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> were the first civilisations to develop their own scripts, in the early Bronze Age; most others reached the end of prehistory in the Iron Age, often through conquest by empires.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>The main source is the archaeological record, studied together with the natural sciences. Archaeologists and physical anthropologists use excavation, geologic and geographic surveys and other scientific analysis, while population geneticists and historical linguists add evidence of their own. The key step is dating, and the most common technique, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/radiocarbon-dating">radiocarbon dating</a>, is one of several that have developed steadily since the nineteenth century.</p><p>Cultures that were written about by others but had no writing of their own are said to have a protohistory. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/celts">Celts</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etruscans">Etruscans</a>, neighbours of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ancient-greece">Ancient Greece</a> and Ancient Rome, are examples, and historians debate how much weight to give the sometimes biased accounts of them in Greek and Roman literature. The protohistory of Ireland is another.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Prehistory” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
